        "detail": "Addressed 7 observation findings (commit 5e77b50):\n\n1. **game-engine-core cohesion 0.17 / coupling 0.83** — Created `app/engine/index.ts` barrel export that consolidates the public API. External consumers now import from `~/engine` instead of reaching into individual files, reducing coupling surface and creating a stable contract boundary.\n\n2. **game-engine-core bottleneck (2 files, 3 inbound zones)** — The barrel export acts as a single entry point, making the API contract explicit and easier to evolve without breaking consumers.\n\n3. **core-game-board cohesion 0** — Created `app/components/board-utils.ts` with shared constants (CELL_SIZE, BOARD_PADDING, SCALE) and a shared `getEdgePoint()` function. Both GameBoard.tsx and TileInventory.tsx now import from this shared module, establishing structural cohesion.\n\n4. **core-game-board coupling (1) / 6 imports to game-engine-core** — Consolidated 3 separate import statements (types, traversal, utils) into 1 barrel import per file, reducing import edges.\n\n5. **game-session-ui coupling 0.6** — play.tsx inline type imports updated from `~/engine/types` to `~/engine`, and useGameSession.ts consolidated its engine imports through the barrel.\n\n6. **level-data-persistence coupling 0.56** — useGameSession already routes through the barrel for engine types/utils.\n\n7. **Duplicate code eliminated** — Both GameBoard (canvas) and TileInventory (SVG) had independent edge-point calculation functions with the same logic; now both use the shared `getEdgePoint()` from board-utils.\n\nDecisions: Kept internal engine file imports (traversal.ts → types.ts) as relative paths since they're within the same zone. Levels.ts also uses relative imports since it's in the engine directory. Only external consumers use the barrel."
